How to make postMessage type-safe in under 350 bytes

Blog post by Pavel Grinchenko and Travis Turner Topics

typed-channel makes postMessage type-safe in about 350 bytes. You define message names and payloads once, and TypeScript checks both sides. The same API works across Web Workers, iframes, tabs, and in-page event buses. Version 0.11 adds cancelable requests: cancel a request, and its handler stops too.

Every frontend project reaches a point where two pieces of code need to exchange data, but don’t share a memory space. For example: a page and a Web Worker, a page and an iframe, a Figma plugin’s UI and its sandbox, or two open browser tabs. The browser gives us postMessage for all of these cases.

postMessage is a good primitive, but you have to write a lot of code around it. And it’s the same code in every project. You need a switch on event.data.type to route messages, the message names are plain strings that TypeScript doesn’t check, and listeners are easy to forget to remove. Sooner or later, you’ll also write a “send a message and wait for the reply” helper with its own ids, its own timeout, and its own bugs.

Evil Martians faced this scenario while building Harmonizer , an accessible color palette generator. Harmonizer runs color calculations in a worker, has an internal event bus, and ships as a Figma plugin. Each of these needed its own messaging code, and we didn’t want to write and maintain the same logic three times. So, we built typed-channel , a small library that covers all three cases with a single typed API for sending and receiving messages.

Earlier versions of typed-channel only handled one-way messages. If one side needed an answer back, you still had to build request/response yourself. The latest release, typed-channel 0.11, added the missing part: requests. A request is a message the sender waits on, it has a typed answer, and an abort signal that also stops the work on the other side.

One-way messages cost around 300 bytes, and request/response is a separate endpoint, so you only pay for it if you use it.

For context, let’s look at the worker case, as it’s the most common. The page sends a job, the worker sends results back. With plain postMessage it looks like this:

const worker = new Worker ( new URL ( "./worker.ts" , import .meta.url), { type: "module" });

worker. addEventListener ( "message" , (event) => {

worker. postMessage ({ type: "generate:colors" , payload: { levels, hues } });

self. addEventListener ( "message" , (event) => {

if (event.data.type === "generate:colors" ) {

for ( const color of calculateColors (event.data.payload)) {

self. postMessage ({ type: "generated:color" , payload: color });

This works, and for a small script it’s fine. The problems crop up when the project grows.

Nothing is typed across the boundary: event.data is any . Message names are strings that TypeScript never checks, so a typo like "generated:colors" instead of "generated:color" compiles and fails at runtime. The payload shape is either undocumented, described in a comment, or defined in a type you import on both sides and cast to by hand.

No single place declares which messages each side sends and receives. The worker accepts one set of messages and sends another, and that contract exists only as the union of every case in every switch . When another developer adds a message, they need to find both sides and keep them in sync.

You have to handle cleanup yourself, because addEventListener doesn’t return an unsubscribe function. To stop listening, you keep a reference to the handler and pass the same one to removeEventListener . In components that mount and unmount, this often causes duplicated handlers and leaked listeners.

Every boundary needs its own copy of this code. An iframe needs nearly the same code, plus event.origin checks and a target origin for postMessage . A BroadcastChannel needs the same code again. An in-page event bus uses new CustomEvent("x", { detail }) to send and…
